INT. FIREHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

A long steel table scarred by forty years of dinners. Pots hang from a rack. A framed photo of a fire engine hangs crooked over the stove. The scanner on the shelf murmurs in a low, sleepy voice.

CAPTAIN LORETTA BAXTER, 50s, iron-grey crop, broad shoulders, stirs a pot of chili. DMITRI VOLKOV, 30s, firefighter, sits at the table in a station t-shirt, peeling an orange. FAYE MCALLISTER, 20s, the probie, mops the floor with great, nervous strokes. CASPIAN ROURKE, 40s, driver, slumps in a chair with his boots on the table. YOLANDA DEVEREAUX, 30s, EMT, cleans her glasses on the hem of her shirt.

LORETTA
Chili in five.

CASPIAN
You said that an hour ago.

LORETTA
It's been four minutes.

DMITRI
She's right. I've been watching.

FAYE
Captain, I finished the floor.

LORETTA
Do it again.

FAYE
But it's finished.

LORETTA
Then do it better.

Faye looks at the mop. At Loretta. Wrings it out. Starts again.

The scanner crackles. A tone. A flat dispatch voice reads out: Engine Six, structure fire, forty-one Maple. Everyone stops.

Chairs scrape. Dmitri drops the orange. Caspian's boots hit the floor. Yolanda shoves her glasses on. Faye flings the mop and it clatters against the stove. Loretta turns off the burner, and in one motion drags the pot to the back.

LORETTA (CONT'D)
Rolling!

They run, boots pounding, through the swinging door and into the bay. The kitchen empties. The pot ticks. The scanner murmurs on to nobody.

A moment passes. Then Faye reappears at the door, panting, mop in hand. She sees the empty room. Sets down the mop gently, with the tenderness of a person who has run out of reasons to be afraid.

FAYE
(to the empty kitchen)
Bring them home.

She follows them out. The scanner crackles again. The chili simmers.
